        {
          "id": 2156488916,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "> Is there any case where the ISO 7816 should be preferred in the MBIM context? I do not think this should be the case but I added this possibility for you.\n\nI'm not sure yet but it might be quite opposite - most of the real-life cases are ISO 7816 ones.\n\nI don't know where is the origins of the CLA come from - is it something UICC-specific, or we can force it in our terminal equipment?\\\nIf the former, here we are - all of these UICCs mentioned by other people presumably are ISO 7816 ones (needs to be confirmed):\\\nhttps://blog.apdu.fr/posts/2019/08/iso-7816-4-spy-using-wireshark/ \\\nhttps://github.com/estkme-group/lpac/issues/118#issuecomment-2169488267\n\n> Given that the CLA byte meaning comes from the ETSI/3GPP spec\n\nThat doesn't seem the case, either:\\\nplease see ISO 7816-4, clause 5.4 \"Class byte\":\\\nhttps://github.com/estkme-group/lpac/issues/118#issuecomment-2169637432\n\n> I think it should be the other way around: add an option to the gsm_sim dissector to call the iso7816 one depending on the CLA value.\n\nIf aforementioned is true, maybe this is still an open question:\\\nif ISO 7816-4 describes \"interindustry\" APDU format/coding, and ETSI TS 102 221 is about \"proprietary\" ones, it might be quite possible ISO 7816 is more \"origin\" standard, more widely represented on practice. But that is just my guess..",
          "author": {
            "id": 1199718,
            "username": "bam80",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Andrey",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7ab86baac98545d1d9105f4a32741596139828706fc4fc7e7905ac689774f54b?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/bam80"
          },
